[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What should "logging" mean here — output you read locally, or records that leave the user's machine? → Console +
[39m     [38;5;246mship to a collector[39m
     [38;5;246m· Which surface needs logging — the browser app, the Node entry point, or both? → Both, shared logger[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Both answers escalate the design, and one of them creates a real problem worth naming now.

  [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a plain [38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m — no module system, everything global. [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m is CommonJS ([38;5;153mrequire[39m).
  There is currently no way to share a single file between them. "Shared logger" therefore isn't a file you drop in;
  it's a packaging decision about this repo, and it's the structural crux of the design. So that's the next question.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how one logger source file gets consumed by both a [38;5;153m<script>[39m-tag browser page and a CommonJS Node process.

  - [1mESM everywhere[0m — write [38;5;153msrc/logger.js[39m as an ES module; change [38;5;153mindex.html[39m to [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m and
    convert [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m/[38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m from [38;5;153mrequire[39m to [38;5;153mimport[39m. Buys you one source file, no build step, no duplication,
    and the modern default. Costs a conversion of the two existing Node files and a [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m.
    Also changes browser semantics: module scripts are deferred and don't create globals, so [38;5;153mapp.js[39m's top-level
    [38;5;153mgetElementById[39m still works but the file stops being a global script. Rules out nothing. Hard to undo cheaply once
    the Node files are converted — though that conversion is small here (two files, three lines).
  - [1mAdd a build step[0m — keep sources as ESM, bundle for the browser with esbuild/Vite. Buys you the same single source
    plus room to grow (minification, a real dep tree for the transport SDK). Costs the first build tooling and
    [38;5;153mnode_modules[39m in a repo that currently has neither, plus a build to run before the page works. This is the option a
    Sentry-style SDK effectively pushes you toward.
  - [1mTwo thin adapters over a shared core[0m — one core file plus a browser shim and a Node shim. Buys you zero changes to
    the existing files and no build. Costs duplication that will drift, and it's the option you'd regret first.

  [1mMy recommendation: ESM everywhere.[0m The repo is four small files with no dependencies; converting them is a few minutes
  and it's the honest version of "shared logger." I'd add a build step only when the collector choice actually requires
  an npm SDK — which is exactly the next question, so we may revisit this together.

